README.rst
    …
      13→beets
      14→=====
      15→
      16→Beets is the media library management system for obsessive music geeks.
      17→
      18→The purpose of beets is to get your music collection right once and for all. It
      19→catalogs your collection, automatically improving its metadata as it goes. It
      20→then provides a suite of tools for manipulating and accessing your music.
      21→
      22→Here's an example of beets' brainy tag corrector doing its thing:
      23→
      24→::
      25→
      26→    $ beet import ~/music/ladytron
      27→    Tagging:
      28→        Ladytron - Witching Hour
      29→    (Similarity: 98.4%)
      30→     * Last One Standing      -> The Last One Standing
      31→     * Beauty                 -> Beauty*2
      32→     * White Light Generation -> Whitelightgenerator
      33→     * All the Way            -> All the Way...
      34→
      35→Because beets is designed as a library, it can do almost anything you can
      36→imagine for your music collection. Via plugins_, beets becomes a panacea:
      37→
      38→- Fetch or calculate all the metadata you could possibly need: `album art`_,
      39→  lyrics_, genres_, tempos_, ReplayGain_ levels, or `acoustic fingerprints`_.
      40→- Get metadata from MusicBrainz_, Discogs_, and Beatport_. Or guess metadata
      41→  using songs' filenames or their acoustic fingerprints.
      42→- `Transcode audio`_ to any format you like.
      43→- Check your library for `duplicate tracks and albums`_ or for `albums that are
      44→  missing tracks`_.
      45→- Clean up crufty tags left behind by other, less-awesome tools.
      46→- Embed and extract album art from files' metadata.
      47→- Browse your music library graphically through a Web browser and play it in any
      48→  browser that supports `HTML5 Audio`_.
      49→- Analyze music files' metadata from the command line.
      50→- Listen to your library with a music player that speaks the MPD_ protocol and
      51→  works with a staggering variety of interfaces.
      52→
      53→If beets doesn't do what you want yet, `writing your own plugin`_ is shockingly
      54→simple if you know a little Python.
    …
